## TL;DR  

1. **Drop Leaflet** – create a plain MapLibre GL JS map (`new maplibre.Map(...)`).  
2. **Serve your data as vector tiles** (e.g. `/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` or a PMTiles archive).  
3. **Write a small style JSON** (or start from a hosted basemap style) that adds:  
   * a **vector source** for your tiles,  
   * a **geojson source** for any tiny static overlays you still want to keep inline,  
   * the corresponding **layers** (fill, line, circle, symbol, …).  
4. **Migrate the Leaflet code** by mapping each Leaflet API call to its MapLibre equivalent (see the “Leaflet-to-MapLibre mapping table” below).  
5. **Add UI/controls** you need (zoom, attribution, layer-switcher) with MapLibre-GL-JS plugins or vanilla HTML – you no longer need Leaflet’s control system.  

The **official migration guide** is the single-source of truth:  

* **Leaflet migration guide** – <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/guides/leaflet-migration-guide/>  

Below is a concrete step-by-step plan, plus a quick “code-translation” table that shows where each common Leaflet construct lives in MapLibre.

---

## 1. Set up a pure MapLibre GL JS map

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>My MapLibre map</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
import {Map} from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.esm.js';

const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY', // any vector basemap style
  center: [ -122.44, 37.76 ],   // lon, lat
  zoom: 12
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*If you already have a basemap style URL (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, your own TileJSON, etc.) just plug it in.*  
All further data layers are added **after** the `style` loads:

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // add your own sources & layers here
});
```

---

## 2. Serve your own vector tiles

| Option | How to host | URL pattern you’ll use in the style |
|--------|-------------|--------------------------------------|
| **Static files** (e.g. on S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages) | `tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` | `https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` |
| **PMTiles archive** (single-file, range-request-enabled) | Serve the `.pmtiles` file via a simple HTTP server (e.g. `pmtiles-server` or `nginx` with `pmtiles` module) | `pmtiles://my-tiles.pmtiles` (MapLibre understands the `pmtiles://` scheme) |
| **Tile server** (TileServer-GL, Tileserver-PHP, t-rex, etc.) | Any HTTP tile server that returns MVT | `https://my-tileserver.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` |

*Generating the tiles* – for a GeoJSON dataset you can use **tippecanoe** (small-to-medium) or **Planetiler** (large / OSM-style). Both output MVT that MapLibre reads natively.

```bash
# tippecanoe example (maxzoom 14, 5-point simplification)
tippecanoe -o mytiles.pmtiles -zg -l mylayer -f mydata.geojson
```

---

## 3. Add the vector source & style it

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Vector source for your tiles
  map.addSource('my-data', {
    type: 'vector',
    // use the same pattern you chose above
    url: 'pmtiles://mytiles.pmtiles'          // or a TileJSON URL, or a direct {z}/{x}/{y} pattern
    // if you have a TileJSON endpoint you can also do:
    // url: 'https://my-tileserver.com/tiles.json'
  });

  // 2️⃣ Example layers – adjust paint/layout to your design
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-polygons',
    type: 'fill',
    source: 'my-data',
    'source-layer': 'mylayer',   // name you gave in tippecanoe / Planetiler
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff6600',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'my-data',
    'source-layer': 'mylayer',
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#ff6600',
      'line-width': 2
    }
  });
});
```

If you still have a few tiny static overlays that never exceed the GeoJSON thresholds, you can keep them as **geojson sources**:

```js
map.addSource('small-overlay', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: '/data/points.geojson'
});
map.addLayer({
  id: 'overlay-points',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'small-overlay',
  paint: {
    'circle-radius': 6,
    'circle-color': '#0066ff'
  }
});
```

---

## 4. Mapping Leaflet code → MapLibre code  

| Leaflet API | What it does | MapLibre equivalent (most common) | Notes / gotchas |
|-------------|--------------|-----------------------------------|-----------------|
| `L.map('id', {center, zoom, layers})` | Creates a map container | `new maplibre.Map({container:'id', center, zoom, style})` | `style` must be a full style JSON (or URL). |
| `L.tileLayer(urlTemplate, options)` | Raster tile source | `map.addSource('my-raster', {type:'raster', tiles:[urlTemplate], tileSize:256, ...options})` + `map.addLayer({id:'my-raster', type:'raster', source:'my-raster'})` | Raster tiles are a *source* + a *layer*; you can also use `raster-dem` for terrain. |
| `L.vectorTileLayer({urlTemplate})` (via plugins) | Vector tile source | `map.addSource('my-vector', {type:'vector', url:'urlTemplate'})` + layers as shown above. |
| `L.geoJSON(geojson, {style, onEachFeature})` | Inline GeoJSON overlay | `map.addSource('my-geojson', {type:'geojson', data:geojson})` + one or more `map.addLayer` calls (type `fill`, `line`, `circle`, `symbol`). Styling is done in the layer’s `paint`/`layout`. Interaction (`onEachFeature`) becomes `map.on('click','source-layer', e=>{…})`. |
| `L.control.zoom()` | Zoom UI | `map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl())` (built-in). |
| `L.control.layers(baseLayers, overlays)` | Layer switcher | No built-in; use a UI library (e.g. **maplibre-gl-layer-switcher** or roll your own with HTML checkboxes that call `map.setLayoutProperty(layerId, 'visibility', 'none'|'visible')`). |
| `L.popup().setLatLng(...).setContent(...).openOn(map)` | Popup | `new maplibregl.Popup().setLngLat(...).setHTML(...).addTo(map)` |
| `map.on('click', fn)` (global click) | Click anywhere | `map.on('click', fn)` – same, but you can also listen on a specific source-layer: `map.on('click','my-polygons', fn)`. |
| `layer.bindTooltip(...)` | Tooltip | `new maplibregl.Popup({closeButton:false, closeOnClick:false}).setLngLat(...).setHTML(...).addTo(map)` – you’ll need to manage show/hide on `mouseenter`/`mouseleave`. |
| `L.marker([lon,lat])` | Point marker | `new maplibregl.Marker().setLngLat([lon,lat]).addTo(map)` – works with any source; you can also render points via a `circle` layer for massive point sets. |
| `L.layerGroup([...])` | Group of layers | No direct group object; you can keep an array of layer IDs and toggle visibility together. |
| `L.control.scale()` | Scale bar | `map.addControl(new maplibregl.ScaleControl({maxWidth:200, unit:'metric'}))`. |
| `L.control.attribution({prefix:false})` | Attribution | `map.addControl(new maplibregl.AttributionControl({compact:true}))`. |
| `L.DomUtil.addClass(el, 'foo')` | DOM helper | Same DOM helpers work; MapLibre does not expose a wrapper, just use native `classList`. |
| `L.CRS.EPSG3857` | Web Mercator projection | MapLibre always uses Web Mercator (EPSG:3857). No need to set. |
| `L.WMS` / `L.TileLayer.WMS` | WMS raster source | `map.addSource('my-wms', {type:'raster', tiles:['https://my-wms?service=WMS&request=GetMap&...&bbox={bbox-epsg-3857}&width=256&height=256'], tileSize:256})`. |
| `L.ImageOverlay(url, bounds)` | Single georeferenced image | `map.addSource('my-image', {type:'image', url, coordinates:[[west,south],[east,south],[east,north],[west,north]]}); map.addLayer({id:'my-image', type:'raster', source:'my-image'});` |

### Quick “copy-paste” migration snippet

```js
// Leaflet
const geo = L.geoJSON(myGeojson, {
  style: {color:'#f00'},
  onEachFeature: (f,l)=>l.bindPopup(f.properties.name)
}).addTo(map);

// MapLibre
map.addSource('my-geojson', {type:'geojson', data:myGeojson});
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-geojson-fill',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'my-geojson',
  paint: {'fill-color':'#f00','fill-opacity':0.3}
});
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-geojson-line',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'my-geojson',
  paint: {'line-color':'#f00','line-width':2}
});
map.on('click','my-geojson-fill', e=>{
  const props = e.features[0].properties;
  new maplibregl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
    .setHTML(`<strong>${props.name}</strong>`)
    .addTo(map);
});
```

