## TL;DR  

1. **Drop Leaflet** – create a single MapLibre GL JS map.  
2. **Add your existing raster tile layers** as `raster` sources in the style.  
3. **Serve your own data as a vector-tile source** (`type: "vector"` or `type: "geojson"` if it’s still tiny).  
4. **Write a style JSON** that references the raster sources, the new vector source, and the layers you need (fills, lines, symbols, …).  
5. **Port the Leaflet code** – most Leaflet calls have a one-to-one MapLibre equivalent (see the “Leaflet → MapLibre mapping table” below).  

All the pieces you need are in the official docs:

* **MapLibre GL JS migration guide** – <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/guides/leaflet-migration-guide/>  
* **Style specification** – <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/>  
* **Source-wiring skill** – <../maplibre-source-wiring/SKILL.md> (how to declare TileJSON, `source-layer`, etc.)  

Below is a concrete step-by-step plan plus a quick “Leaflet → MapLibre” cheat-sheet.

---

## 1. Decide the source type for each piece of data

| What you have now | Recommended MapLibre source type | Why |
|-------------------|----------------------------------|-----|
| **Base map raster tiles** (e.g. XYZ, WMTS) | `raster` source | Same format, just declared in the style. |
| **Base map vector tiles** (if you switch to a vector basemap) | `vector` source | Gives you crisp labels & the ability to restyle the basemap. |
| **Small GeoJSON overlays** (< 2 MB / < 5 k features) | `geojson` source (inline or URL) | No tile server needed, still fast. |
| **Medium-large or ever-growing data** (≥ 5 k features, > 5 MB) | **Your own vector tiles** (`type: "vector"`). Generate them once (tippecanoe, Planetiler, etc.) and host them (static files, PMTiles, Cloud-optimized tile server). | Scales, lets you change style without rebuilding tiles, supports feature queries. |

> **Rule of thumb** – If the data fits comfortably in the “sweet-spot” for GeoJSON (≤ 2 MB, ≤ 5 k features) you can keep it as GeoJSON. Anything larger should be tiled.

---

## 2. Build a minimal style JSON

A style is just a JSON object that tells MapLibre *where* to get data and *how* to draw it.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap-raster": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://my.tileserver.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "attribution": "© My Tiles"
    },

    "my-data": {
      "type": "vector",               // or "geojson" if you stay small
      "tiles": ["https://my.tileserver.org/data/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"],
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 14
    }
  },

  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "basemap",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "basemap-raster"
    },

    // Example: draw polygons from the vector source
    {
      "id": "my-polygons",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "my-data",
      "source-layer": "my_layer_name",   // name inside the .pbf
      "paint": {
        "fill-color": "#0080ff",
        "fill-opacity": 0.4
      }
    },

    // Example: draw points as circles
    {
      "id": "my-points",
      "type": "circle",
      "source": "my-data",
      "source-layer": "my_layer_name",
      "paint": {
        "circle-radius": 6,
        "circle-color": "#ff6600"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*Save this as `style.json` and point MapLibre at it:*  

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // <div id="map"></div>
  style: '/style.json',      // URL or inline object
  center: [lon, lat],
  zoom: 12
});
```

All you need to change later is the `sources` block – you can add more raster layers, a `raster-dem` for terrain, etc., without touching the rest of the style.

---

## 3. Replace the Leaflet map creation

| Leaflet | MapLibre GL JS |
|---------|----------------|
| `L.map('map').setView([lat, lng], zoom)` | `new maplibregl.Map({container:'map', style:'/style.json', center:[lng, lat], zoom:zoom})` |
| `L.tileLayer(urlTemplate, options).addTo(map)` | Declared as a `raster` source + a `raster` layer in the style (see JSON above). |
| `L.geoJSON(geojson, {onEachFeature, pointToLayer, style}).addTo(map)` | `map.addSource('my-geojson', {type:'geojson', data:geojson});` then `map.addLayer({id:'my-layer', type:'fill'|'circle'|'line', source:'my-geojson', paint:…});` |
| `layer.on('click', fn)` | `map.on('click','layer-id', fn)` – the event object contains `e.features` (array of clicked features). |
| `L.popup().setLatLng(...).setContent(...).openOn(map)` | `new maplibregl.Popup().setLngLat(e.lngLat).setHTML(...).addTo(map);` |
| `L.control.scale()` | `new maplibregl.ScaleControl().addTo(map);` |
| `L.control.layers(baseLayers, overlays)` | No direct equivalent – you build a UI yourself (e.g. a simple `<select>` that calls `map.setStyle(...)` or toggles layer visibility with `map.setLayoutProperty('layer-id','visibility','none'|'visible')`). |

**Key differences to keep in mind**

* **Layers are defined *once* in the style** – you don’t call `addLayer` repeatedly for every feature.  
* **Feature queries are done via the map object**, not via a Leaflet layer reference.  
* **MapLibre renders everything on a WebGL canvas**, so CSS-based popups must be attached with the built-in `Popup` class (or you can overlay a regular HTML element positioned with `map.project`).  

---

## 4. Port interaction logic (clicks, hover, pop-ups)

### Example: show a popup with a property from a vector tile

```js
map.on('click', 'my-polygons', (e) => {
  const props = e.features[0].properties;
  new maplibregl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
    .setHTML(`<strong>${props.name}</strong><br/>${props.description}`)
    .addTo(map);
});
```

*If you previously used `L.geoJSON`’s `onEachFeature` to bind a popup, the same logic lives in the `click` handler above.*

### Hover style (change fill opacity while mouse is over)

```js
let hoveredId = null;

map.on('mousemove', 'my-polygons', (e) => {
  if (e.features.length) {
    if (hoveredId) {
      map.setFeatureState({source:'my-data', sourceLayer:'my_layer_name', id:hoveredId}, {hover:false});
    }
    hoveredId = e.features[0].id;
    map.setFeatureState({source:'my-data', sourceLayer:'my_layer_name', id:hoveredId}, {hover:true});
  }
});

map.on('mouseleave', 'my-polygons', () => {
  if (hoveredId) {
    map.setFeatureState({source:'my-data', sourceLayer:'my_layer_name', id:hoveredId}, {hover:false});
    hoveredId = null;
  }
});

/* In the style, use the feature state to drive paint */
{
  "id": "my-polygons",
  "type": "fill",
  "source": "my-data",
  "source-layer": "my_layer_name",
  "paint": {
    "fill-color": "#0080ff",
    "fill-opacity": [
      "case",
      ["boolean", ["feature-state", "hover"], false],
      0.8,
      0.4
    ]
  }
}
```

---

## 5. Serve your own vector tiles (quick-start)

You said you “want to serve our own vector tiles”. The **choice of source type** is already settled (`type: "vector"`). How you *host* them is outside the scope of this skill, but the usual options are:

| Hosting option | When it makes sense |
|----------------|---------------------|
| **Static `.pbf` files on a CDN** (e.g. `https://cdn.example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`) | Small-to-medium regional datasets, low traffic, cheap. |
| **PMTiles** (single `.pmtiles` archive) + `pmtiles` JS client or Cloudflare Worker | Offline bundles, or you want a single file for distribution. |
| **Tile server (TileServer-GL, Tileserver-PHP, t-rex, etc.)** | You need on-the-fly tile generation, dynamic styling, or you already have a tile-server stack. |
| **Cloud-native services (MapTiler Cloud, AWS Open Data, Azure Maps)** | Global scale, high availability, built-in CORS & rate-limiting. |

All of those expose the same URL pattern (`{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`) that MapLibre expects, so you don’t need to change the style JSON later.

---

## 6. Optional: keep Leaflet *just* for UI widgets

If you still love Leaflet’s UI controls (layer switcher, easy-to-style legends, etc.) you can embed a **MapLibre GL map as a Leaflet layer** using the plugin **maplibre-gl-leaflet**:

```js
const gl = L.maplibreGL({
  style: '/style.json',
  attributionControl: false
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

But you said you “rather not keep Leaflet around just to host a second map”, so the recommended path is **to drop Leaflet entirely** and use MapLibre’s own controls (`NavigationControl`, `ScaleControl`, custom HTML UI) instead.

---

